sections.s2_technical:
Serializable is a marker interface with no methods; the engine inspects the object with reflection and automatically writes every non-static, non-transient field, and on read it allocates the object without running any constructor and fills the fields. You can customize it with optional private writeObject and readObject methods, but by default it is fully automatic.

Externalizable extends Serializable and declares two public methods you must implement:

```java
public void writeExternal(java.io.ObjectOutput out) throws java.io.IOException;
public void readExternal(java.io.ObjectInput in) throws java.io.IOException, ClassNotFoundException;
```

In writeExternal you explicitly write each value you want, and in readExternal you explicitly read them back in the same order. Nothing is written automatically, so if you forget a field, it is simply absent. Here is the shape in finance code:

```java
public void writeExternal(ObjectOutput out) throws IOException {
    out.writeUTF(accountId);            // you choose the order
    out.writeUTF(balance.toPlainString());
}
public void readExternal(ObjectInput in) throws IOException {
    this.accountId = in.readUTF();      // must match the write order
    this.balance = new java.math.BigDecimal(in.readUTF());
}
```

The second big difference is the constructor lifecycle. With Serializable, deserialization does not call any constructor; it allocates the object directly. With Externalizable, deserialization first calls the public no-argument constructor to create a blank instance, then calls readExternal to fill it. That is why an Externalizable class must have an accessible no-argument constructor; without one, deserialization throws InvalidClassException.

The differences in summary, stated in sentences: Serializable is automatic and reflection-based while Externalizable is manual and code-based; Serializable runs no constructor on read while Externalizable runs the no-argument constructor; Serializable writes all non-transient fields while Externalizable writes only what you code; and with Externalizable the transient keyword is irrelevant because you control every field yourself.

A senior point is when to choose Externalizable. It can be faster and more compact because you skip reflection and write a tight format, and it gives precise control for custom or legacy wire formats. But it is more error-prone, since a mismatch between write and read order silently corrupts data, and it must be maintained by hand whenever fields change. In modern banking systems, teams usually prefer Serializable for internal caches or, more often, a schema format like Protobuf or Avro for anything crossing a service boundary, reserving Externalizable for rare cases that truly need hand-tuned control.

